\DOC ORTHOGONAL_COMPONENTS_BYTES128_TAC

\TYPE {ORTHOGONAL_COMPONENTS_BYTES128_TAC : tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Proves that two 128-bit byte-memory components at distinct addresses are
orthogonal, closing the goal.

\DESCRIBE
Takes no arguments. Applied to a goal whose conclusion is a single
orthogonality statement about a pair of 128-bit byte components, each at a base
pointer possibly offset by a word-valued constant, it closes the goal:
{
   A ?- orthogonal_components t u
   ================================  ORTHOGONAL_COMPONENTS_BYTES128_TAC
}

\FAILURE
Fails, with a matching failure raised by an internal backchaining step,
whenever the conclusion is an orthogonality claim about components that are not
both of the 128-bit byte kind — for instance when one or both sides are
components of a different width, a different component family, or of a
non-fixed size.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces the goal is closed, never transformed or split, and
every input goal had no assumptions and a conclusion that was a single
orthogonality statement about such a pair of components.

\ENDDOC
